\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Selection vs.\ correction in continuous action spaces.}
In contrast to LLM test-time scaling, where selection among candidates drives improvement~\cite{snell2024scaling}, our decomposition reveals that selection alone provides no significant benefit in low-dimensional continuous action spaces ($d{=}6$): $K{=}10$ flow-matching samples cluster too tightly for selection to discriminate.
Learned correction is the active mechanism, but multi-sampling is an enabling condition that provides a centered starting point within the CRM's bounded correction radius; at $K{=}1$, correction alone falls below the VLA-only baseline (10.3\% vs.\ 11.6\%).
Transferring test-time compute ideas to embodied domains requires rethinking the selection-correction balance.
